            <td data-label="Middle drop year as a share">{row.drop}</td>
            <td data-label="What fits">{row.fit}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    A degree does not fix low pay by itself. Regulated routes such as nursing, pharmacy, physiotherapy and clinical psychology need the formal degree and licence, and no course on the internet replaces that. For data and bioinformatics skills, sample free YouTube and reputable low-cost courses first, and pay only for structure, feedback or a credential an employer values. Certificates alone are weak proof. Work you can show is strong proof.
  </p>

  <h2 id="skill-stack">The high-value skill stack that lifts any lane</h2>

  <p>
    Use one core skill, one multiplier and visible proof. This is how a lane turns into a high-income skill portfolio instead of a job title.
  </p>

  <div class="stack-grid">
    <div class="stack-card">
      <div class="stack-card__label">Core skill</div>
      <p>Lab method, pharmacy practice, research method, data handling or patient care, depending on your lane.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack-card">
      <div class="stack-card__label">Multiplier skill</div>
      <p>Clear English and local-language explanation, documentation quality, spreadsheet and SQL basics, or health-data fluency. You do not need to become a coder.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack-card">
      <div class="stack-card__label">Proof</div>
      <p>A lab-skills log, a dataset analysis, a patient-education handout or a literature review, shown on one platform that suits you.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    AI helps in a specific way. It speeds up literature searches, first drafts and data cleaning, so a student who learns to use it and check it produces more in the same hours. It does not replace lab judgment, clinical accountability or the patient in front of you. The pressure is real in medical coding and transcription, and lower in bedside care, lab method and counselling.
  </p>

  <p>
    Stage it. Now, while studying: use AI to summarise papers you then read yourself, and keep a short log of what it got wrong. Later, as tools and adoption mature: build a domain workflow such as a cleaned dataset or a compliance checklist that saves someone hours. Tool familiarity alone does not raise pay, and no multiplier is guaranteed. If you want help sequencing this, <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">continuous career guidance</a> is designed around building a high-value skill portfolio, not a one-time session.
  </p>

